  "result": "An Interpretability-Guided Framework for Responsible Synthetic Data Generation in Emotional Text (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16132v1)\nciteturn6692view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6689view1\",\"lineno\":69}); Total lines: 375\nL66: Despite these advances, explainable AI techniques remain underexplored for guiding responsible synthetic data generation. In particular, Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) (cite55†Lundberg and Lee 2017 ) is widely used in classification tasks (cite56†Kong et al. 2022 ; cite57†Hailemariam et al. 2023 ; cite58†Kim et al. 2024 ) but its role in LLM data augmentation for emotion recognition remains insufficiently studied.\nL67: Moreover, most prior work applies interpretability only indirectly through post-hoc filtering (cite59†Wu and Huang 2022 ) and feature modification (cite60†Mersha et al. 2025 ), and no prior work has directly integrated SHAP-derived feature importance into prompt construction for text generation.\nL68: We address this gap by using token-level SHAP scores to guide LLMs toward generating emotionally aligned synthetic text, particularly in low-resource settings.\nL69: Our contributions include: (1) an interpretability-guided augmentation framework (Figure cite61†1 ) that conditions LLM generation on feature importance; (2) an evaluation of three augmentation strategies––real data expansion, SHAP-guided generation, and naïve generation––across varying data regimes; (3) and a linguistic analysis discussing a fundamental trade-off, where synthetic data exhibits reduced lexical diversity but amplified emotion-discriminative features that benefit minority class performance.\nL70: ## Methods and Experimental Design\nL71: We evaluated six traditional ML algorithms using the TweetEval dataset (Appendix cite20†B ): K-Nearest Neighbors, Random Forest, XGBoost, AdaBoost, HistGradientBoosting, and LightGBM. Using real data expansion as the baseline augmentation strategy, we tested each classifier across all data increments and over several folds to identify the algorithm that would provide the most reliable performance benchmarks.\nL72: XGBoost consistently demonstrated the highest mean F1-score (Table cite62†A1 ) while maintaining stable performance patterns throughout the augmentation process. This stability was crucial to ensure that the observed performance differences between augmentation strategies could be attributed to their effectiveness rather than to model-specific inconsistencies. We therefore used XGBoost exclusively for all subsequent experiments.\nL73: ### Data Augmentation Strategies\nL74: #### Real Data Expansion\nL75: \nL76: Real data expansion represents the traditional approach to improving model performance through the acquisition of additional manually labeled data. The candidate real samples are the remaining observations after the removal of the holdout set and the seed data. This strategy serves as the baseline for evaluating whether synthetic data can provide comparable benefits to authentic data while avoiding the resource constraints associated with manual annotation and extraction costs.\nL77: #### Interpretability-guided Synthetic Data Generation\nL78: This method combines Shapley analysis with TF-IDF differential scoring to identify emotion-relevant keywords. We trained the classifier on the seed data and applied SHAP to quantify feature importance. We then calculated the mean TF-IDF for each feature within a target emotion and subtracted the mean TF-IDF values of all other emotions (e.g., for anger, we compute average TF-IDF for anger tweets minus the average for joy, optimism, and sadness combined).\nL79: This differential scoring reveals which terms are discriminatively associated with specific emotions, where positive differences indicate words more frequent in the target emotion and negative differences highlight words more characteristic of other emotions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn Interpretability-Guided Framework for Responsible Synthetic Data Generation in Emotional Text (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16132v1)\nciteturn6692view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6689view1\",\"lineno\":105}); Total lines: 375\nL99: For anger (42% of data), all strategies show stable performance with marginal differences (Figure cite65†4 ), indicating that adequate training data reduces sensitivity to augmentation quality. This frequency-performance relationship reveals a clear pattern where SHAP-guided augmentation provides maximal value for minority classes with limited authentic examples, while majority classes show robustness across strategies.\nL100: Similar patterns emerged for sadness and joy (Appendix cite28†F ), with SHAP-guided approaches maintaining performance comparable to real data while naïve generation degraded consistently.\nL101: Figure 4: All strategies show stable performance for majority class anger (42%), demonstrating that adequate baseline data reduces augmentation sensitivity.\nL102: ### Model Performance on Different Data Regimes\nL103: \nL104: Figure cite66†5 shows a shift in the effectiveness of SHAP-guided augmentation under reduced seed data. At the 500-sample baseline, SHAP-guided augmentation closely aligns with real data expansion and consistently outperforms naïve augmentation across all increments. However, its gains taper off with larger increments and eventually falls behind real data, unlike in the 1,000-sample baseline (Figure cite63†2 ), where SHAP-guided data consistently leads.\nL105: Figure 5: With 500-sample baseline, SHAP-guided generation achieves parity with real data initially but plateaus at higher increments, demonstrating that interpretability-guided generation requires adequate seed data for sustained effectiveness.\nL106: This downward trend becomes even more pronounced at the 100-sample baseline (Figure cite67†6 ), where SHAP-guided augmentation offers only marginal improvement over naïve generation and remains consistently below real data. These findings underscore that while interpretability-guided generation is highly effective, its performance depends on a minimally sufficient dataset to extract meaningful emotional patterns. When this threshold is not met, its advantage diminishes.\nL107: Thus, even with interpretability, LLM-based augmentation complements rather than replaces real data in extremely low-resource scenarios.\nL108: Figure 6: At 100-sample baseline, SHAP-guided generation marginally outperforms naïve but remains below real data throughout, demonstrating that interpretability guidance requires minimally sufficient seed data to provide any advantage.\nL109: ### Linguistic Analysis of Synthetically Generated Emotional Text\nL110: Both the SHAP-guided and naïve approaches exhibit lower lexical diversity than real data (TTR: SHAP 0.133, Naïve 0.143 vs. Real 0.241, Table cite68†1 ), with SHAP-guided showing lowest diversity due to focused repetition of emotion-discriminative features. This concentration amplifies salient patterns beneficial for minority classes but risks overfitting to specific constructions, and is particularly problematic for social media’s evolving linguistics.\nL111: Table 1: Lexical diversity (TTR) and overlap (Jaccard) metrics. TTR measures vocabulary richness within each dataset (higher = more diverse). Jaccard measures lexical overlap between real and synthetic data (higher = more similar vocabularies).\nL112: Metric  | Real Data  | SHAP-Guided  | Naïve\nL113: Type-Token Ratio  | 0.241  | 0.133  | 0.143\nL114: Jaccard Similarity (vs. Real Data):\nL115: ---\nL116: Anger  | —  | 0.209  | 0.184\nL117: Optimism  | —  | 0.247  | 0.183\nL118: Sadness  | —  | 0.232  | 0.210\nL119: Joy  | —  | 0.188  | 0.171\nL120: SHAP-guided generation also achieves higher lexical overlap with real data across all emotions (Jaccard: 0.188-0.247 vs. naïve: 0.171-0.210), with optimism showing strongest alignment despite being the minority class.\nL121: Syntactic analysis (Figure cite69†7 ) further reveals that SHAP-guided data overproduces content-rich patterns (ADJ-NOUN, NOUN-VERB) that convey clearer emotional cues, but both synthetic approaches underproduce personal voice patterns (PRON-AUX, PRON-VERB), reducing first-person perspectives that are characteristic of authentic social media text (extended analysis and examples in Appendix cite31†G ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAs If We’ve Met Before: LLMs Exhibit Certainty in Recognizing Seen Files (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.15192v2)\nciteturn6692view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6689view2\",\"lineno\":238}); Total lines: 570\nL222: Manhattan distance [cite110†67 ] is calculated as the sum of the absolute differences between corresponding elements of two probability distributions $P$ and $Y=\\{y,y,\\dots,y\\}_{n}$:\nL223: \nL224:  | $$D_{\\text{Manhattan}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}|p_{i}-y|.$$  |\nL225: \nL226: Euclidean distance [cite110†67 ] is calculated as the square root of the sum of squared differences between corresponding elements of two probability distributions $P$ and $Y$:\nL227: \nL228:  | $$D_{\\text{Euclidean}}=\\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(p_{i}-y)^{2}}.$$  |\nL229: Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) [cite111†68 ] is calculated as the negative sum of the product of the true labels and the logarithm of the predicted probabilities for each class:\nL230: \nL231:  | $$\\text{NLL}=-\\sum_{i=1}^{n}y\\log(p_{i}).$$  |\nL232: \nL233: Chebyshev distance [cite112†69 ] is calculated as the maximum absolute difference between corresponding elements of two probability distributions $P$ and $Y$:\nL234: \nL235:  | $$D_{\\text{Chebyshev}}=\\max_{i=1,2,\\dots,n}|p_{i}-y|.$$  |\nL236: \nL237: Kullback-Leibler Divergence [cite109†66 ] is calculated as:\nL238:  | $$D_{\\text{FU-KL}}(P\\|Y)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}\\log\\left(\\frac{p_{i}}{y}\\right).$$  |\nL239: \nL240: In summary, for each given snippet, an uncertainty estimation LLM generates a set of predicted probabilities, from which 13 distinct uncertainty metrics can be derived. In our framework, we utilize three such LLMs, resulting in a total of 39 uncertainty metrics per snippet.\nL241: ### IV-C Seen File Detection\nL242: In this section, we focus on constructing a global representation of file-level uncertainty and utilize it to perform unsupervised clustering on the Suspected Seen Dataset. Specifically, we standardize the snippet-level uncertainty metrics and apply Principal Component Analysis (PCA) for dimensionality reduction, resulting in a 10-dimensional vector per snippet.\nL243: Subsequently, we utilize max pooling to aggregate the most informative features across all snippets, yielding a concise 10-dimensional uncertainty vector that characterizes each file. These vectors serve as input for the final clustering using an unsupervised learning algorithm. The detailed process is as follows:\nL244: #### IV-C 1 Feature Engineering\nL245: \nL246: Feature standardization is a critical preprocessing step in feature engineering. It transforms each feature to have a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1 using the formula:\nL247: \nL248:  | $$z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}.$$  |\nL249: \nL250: We standardize 39 uncertainty metrics per snippet to ensure that all features are on a comparable scale, which facilitates fair and effective model training.\nL251: Subsequently, we apply Principal Component Analysis (PCA) [cite113†70 ] to compress the standardized features. PCA is a classical unsupervised dimensionality reduction technique that transforms the original high-dimensional features into a set of orthogonal components. These components are ranked according to the amount of variance they capture, selecting the top components that retain the most critical information while discarding redundant dimensions.\nL252: In our work, the 39 original uncertainty metrics are compressed into 10 principal components.\nL253: After constructing snippet-level uncertainty features, we further obtain a global uncertainty representation for each file by applying a max-pooling operation. Specifically, given the 10-dimensional uncertainty feature of all snippets within a file, the maximum value for each dimension is extracted to form a new 10-dimensional uncertainty feature vector.\nL254: #### IV-C 2 Unsupervised Clustering Algorithms\nL255: \nL256: We rely on unsupervised clustering algorithms to classify the suspected seen files. Based on data similarity, these algorithms divides the files into two clusters. The one with higher uncertainty is treated as representing unseen files, and the one with lower uncertainty as seen files. In this work, we apply three widely used unsupervised clustering algorithms:\nL257: \nL258:   * •\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAs If We’ve Met Before: LLMs Exhibit Certainty in Recognizing Seen Files (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.15192v2)\nciteturn6692view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6689view2\",\"lineno\":286}); Total lines: 570\nL262: Hierarchical Clustering (HC) [cite114†71 ]. HC is a widely used unsupervised clustering algorithms that builds a tree-like structure (dendrogram) to represent the hierarchical relationships within the data. In our work, we use a bottom-up agglomerative hierarchical clustering method. Data similarity is calculated using Euclidean distance, which measures the straight-line distance between points in multidimensional space.\nL263: For cluster merging, we apply Ward’s method, which merges the pair of clusters that results in the smallest increase in within-cluster variance.\nL264:   * •\nL265: \nL266: K-Means [cite115†72 ]. K-Means is a clustering algorithm that partitions data points into $K$ clusters, calculates the centroid of each cluster, and iteratively refines the cluster assignments. In our work, we set $K=2$ to partition the files into two clusters, representing seen and unseen files.\nL267: ## V Evaluation\nL268: \nL269: We explore the effectiveness of CopyCheck in detecting seen files from the Suspected Seen Dataset by seeking answers to the following three research questions:\nL270: \nL271:   * •\nL272: \nL273: RQ1: How effective is CopyCheck in detecting seen files?\nL274: \nL275:   * •\nL276: \nL277: RQ2: Can CopyCheck outperform the state-of-the-art tool?\nL278: \nL279:   * •\nL280: \nL281: RQ3: Can CopyCheck generalize effectively to different data sources and LLMs with different architectures?\nL282: \nL283:   * •\nL284: \nL285: RQ4: How effective is CopyCheck in black-box LLM settings?\nL286: ### V-A Evaluation Setup\nL287: \nL288: We implement CopyCheck using the Pytorch framework and two GeForce A100 40GB GPUs driven by CUDA.\nL289: #### V-A 1 Dataset\nL290: \nL291: Our source data includes 200 books collected from two sources. The first source is the BookMIA [cite59†19 ] dataset, which includes 50 books seen by the target LLM and 50 books unseen by it. The second source comprises 100 books published after January 2023, which we manually collected in collaboration with our industry partners to serve as unseen by the target LLM. Consequently, our source data includes 50 books considered seen and 150 considered unseen by the target LLM.\nL292: We construct four datasets, each containing 100 books, with varying numbers of books seen by the target LLM set to 10, 20, 30, and 40, respectively. The construction of these datasets is described in §cite13†III-B1 .\nL293: #### V-A 2 Target LLMs\nL294: In RQ1 and RQ3, we select three widely used open-source LLMs that meet the requirements of the BookMIA dataset: LLaMA 7B, LLaMA2 7B, and GPT-J 6B. All models are publicly available on Hugging Face [cite116†73 ] under the repositories “huggyllama/llama-7b”, “meta-llama/Llama-2-7b”, and “EleutherAI/gpt-j-6b”. In RQ2, we select the same LLMs used in  [cite65†25 ], namely OpenLLaMA 3B and OpenLLaMA 7B.\nL295: These models are also available on Hugging Face under the repositories “openlm-research/open_llama_3b” and “openlm-research/open_llama_7b”.\nL296: #### V-A 3 Baseline Approaches\nL297: \nL298: To demonstrate the effectiveness of CopyCheck, we conduct a comparative evaluation against four state-of-the-art approaches. An overview of these baselines is provided in Table cite117†I , detailing their underlying methodologies.\nL299: \nL300: TABLE I: Details of baseline approaches.\nL301: Approach  | Description\nL302: --- | ---\nL303: Prob-Tool [cite59†19 ]  | Computes the mean of the bottom K% token probabilities as an estimate of prior exposure.\nL304: Neighbor-LOSS [cite60†20 ]  | Measures the relative deviation of a sample’s loss from its nearest neighbours to detect exposure.\nL305: Neighbor-PPL [cite60†20 ]  | Same as Neighbor-LOSS, but uses perplexity to measure deviation.\nL306: Meeus et al. [cite65†25 ]  | Queries the LLM for token-level probabilities, converting them into handcrafted features for a supervised meta-classifier. Requires substantial labeled data for training. Finally, an unsupervised clustering algorithm is employed to discriminate between seen and unseen files.\nL307: #### V-A 4 Evaluation Metrics\nL308: \nL309: We evaluate the performance of CopyCheck using standard classification metrics: True Positives (TP), False Positives (FP), False Negatives (FN), and True Negatives (TN).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAs If We’ve Met Before: LLMs Exhibit Certainty in Recognizing Seen Files (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.15192v2)\nciteturn6692view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6689view2\",\"lineno\":450}); Total lines: 570\nL444: ### V-E RQ4: Effectiveness in Black-Box Setting\nL445: \nL446: While our main experiments focus on the white-box setting, where CopyCheck has full access to model parameters enabling uncertainty estimation methods (BLoB, MCD, and Ensemble), we also explore a black-box variant where only API-level log-probabilities can be queried without parameter access.\nL447: To perform uncertainty quantification in this black-box setting, we construct $N=10$ semantically varied prompts for each text snippet, asking whether the model has ”seen” the text before, with outputs restricted to binary responses (0 or 1). We then query the black-box model and collect the log-probabilities of tokens ”0” and ”1” across these prompts to derive uncertainty metrics, as described in §cite20†IV-B .\nL448: The calculated uncertainty metrics then undergo the same aggregation and clustering procedures as the white-box pipeline described in §cite21†IV-C .\nL449: TABLE VI: Evaluating the effectiveness of CopyCheck using GPT-3.5-Turbo-Instruct in detecting seen files. L stands for the length of WIKI sample.\nL450: WikiMIA 24  | GPT 3.5 Turbo Instruct\nL451: L  | Tool  | Acc.(%)  | TP  | FP  | FN  | TN\nL452: 32  | Neighbor-PPL  | 50.1  | 776  | 775  | 3  | 4\nL453: Neighbor-LOSS  | 52.3  | 538  | 503  | 241  | 276\nL454: CopyCheck  | 64.6  | 589  | 362  | 190  | 417\nL455: 64  | Neighbor-PPL  | 50.1  | 3  | 2  | 695  | 696\nL456: Neighbor-LOSS  | 50.1  | 697  | 694  | 1  | 4\nL457: CopyCheck  | 66.8  | 515  | 281  | 183  | 417\nL458: 128  | Neighbor-PPL  | 50.0  | 0  | 0  | 286  | 286\nL459: Neighbor-LOSS  | 50.0  | 0  | 0  | 286  | 286\nL460: CopyCheck  | 66.3  | 197  | 104  | 89  | 182\nL461: 256  | Neighbor-PPL  | 51.1  | 28  | 26  | 65  | 67\nL462: Neighbor-LOSS  | 53.2  | 37  | 31  | 56  | 62\nL463: CopyCheck  | 68.8  | 67  | 32  | 26  | 61\nL464: Table cite127†VI summarizes the results under an API-only black-box setting using GPT-3.5-Turbo-Instruct. Despite the absence of internal access, CopyCheck achieves up to 68.8% accuracy on WikiMIA-24 dataset, which is available on Hugging Face under the repository “wjfu99/WikiMIA”, outperforming probability-based baseline methods by more than 15 points. These findings demonstrate that uncertainty-based copyright detection remains feasible under API-only black-box access.\nL465: ## VI Discussion\nL466: File-level Detection. Our work operates at the file level, treating each file as either entirely seen or entirely unseen by the target LLM. This design choice aligns with established practices in file-level membership inference research [cite65†25 ]. While we ensure that only books published after the target LLM’s release date are classified as unseen, we acknowledge the possibility of partial exposure through snippets within these files.\nL467: However, such quoted content from older sources constitutes only a minimal fraction (less than 5%) of the total content in unseen files. To preserve data integrity, our approach maintains focus on file-level detection without removing these incidental quotes. Future research could extend this framework by incorporating snippet-level uncertainty analysis to detect and characterize partial exposure patterns within individual files.\nL468: Temporal shift of Dataset. To further examine whether CopyCheck ’s uncertainty signal is affected by temporal or domain differences, we conduct additional experiments on the ArXiv dataset, where each configuration mixes 2023 papers (non-members) with member papers from nearby years (2018 or 2022). As shown in Table cite126†V , CopyCheck achieves consistently high accuracy for both year settings, comparable to that on BookMIA.\nL469: These results confirm that CopyCheck captures genuine membership exposure rather than spurious time-dependent artifacts, demonstrating robustness across domains and publication periods.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.13182v2] Evaluating Large Language Models for Diacritic Restoration in Romanian Texts: A Comparative Study (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.13182v2)\nciteturn6692view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6689view3\",\"lineno\":36}); Total lines: 202\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite53†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite1†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org L5: \nL6: cite54†Image: arxiv logo > cite3†cs > arXiv:2511.13182v2\nL7: \nL8: cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite5†Advanced Search L9: \nL10:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite55†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2511.13182v2 (cs)\nL31: \nL32:  This paper has been withdrawn by Mihai Nadas\nL33: \nL34: [Submitted on 17 Nov 2025 (cite9†v1 ), revised 18 Nov 2025 (this version, v2), latest version 21 Nov 2025 (cite10†v3 )]\nL35: # Title:Evaluating Large Language Models for Diacritic Restoration in Romanian Texts: A Comparative Study\nL36: \nL37: Authors:cite11†Mihai Dan Nadas , cite12†Laura Diosan L38: \nL39: View a PDF of the paper titled Evaluating Large Language Models for Diacritic Restoration in Romanian Texts: A Comparative Study, by Mihai Dan Nadas and 1 other authors\nL40: \nL41: No PDF available, click to view other formats\nL42: > Abstract:Automatic diacritic restoration is crucial for text processing in languages with rich diacritical marks, such as Romanian. This study evaluates the performance of several large language models (LLMs) in restoring diacritics in Romanian texts.\nL43: Using a comprehensive corpus, we tested models including OpenAI's GPT-3.5, GPT-4, GPT-4o, Google's Gemini 1.0 Pro, Meta's Llama 2 and Llama 3, MistralAI's Mixtral 8x7B Instruct, airoboros 70B, and OpenLLM-Ro's RoLlama 2 7B, under multiple prompt templates ranging from zero-shot to complex multi-shot instructions.\nL44: Results show that models such as GPT-4o achieve high diacritic restoration accuracy, consistently surpassing a neutral echo baseline, while others, including Meta's Llama family, exhibit wider variability. These findings highlight the impact of model architecture, training data, and prompt design on diacritic restoration performance and outline promising directions for improving NLP tools for diacritic-rich languages.\nL45: Comments: The original submission contained metadata errors and requires correction. A revised and complete version will be submitted as a replacement\nL46: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL47: Cite as: cite13†arXiv:2511.13182 [cs.CL]\nL48:   (or  cite14†arXiv:2511.13182v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL49:   cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.13182†doi.org L50: \nL51: Focus to learn more\nL52: \nL53:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL54: ## Submission history\nL55: \nL56: From: Mihai Nadas [cite16†view email ]\nL57: cite9†[v1] Mon, 17 Nov 2025 09:43:54 UTC (3,433 KB)\nL58: [v2] Tue, 18 Nov 2025 13:16:32 UTC (1 KB) (withdrawn)\nL59: cite10†[v3] Fri, 21 Nov 2025 14:13:16 UTC (2,737 KB)\nL60: \nL61: Full-text links:\nL62: ## Access Paper:\nL63: \nL64:  View a PDF of the paper titled Evaluating Large Language Models for Diacritic Restoration in Romanian Texts: A Comparative Study, by Mihai Dan Nadas and 1 other authors\nL65: \nL66:   * Withdrawn\nL67: \nL68: No license for this version due to withdrawn\nL69: \nL70: Current browse context:\nL71: \nL72: cs.CL\nL73: \nL74: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L75: \nL76: cite19†new |  cite20†recent | cite21†2025-11 L77: \nL78: Change to browse by:\nL79: \nL80: cite22†cs L81: ### References & Citations\nL82: \nL83:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L84:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L85:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L86: \nL87: export BibTeX citation Loading...\nL88: \nL89: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL90: \nL91: ×\nL92: \nL93: loading...\nL94: \nL95: Data provided by:\nL96: \nL97: ### Bookmark\nL98: \nL99: cite26† †www.bibsonomy.org cite27† †reddit.com L100: \nL101: Bibliographic Tools\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSDA: Steering-Driven Distribution Alignment for Open LLMs without Fine-Tuning (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16324v1)\nciteturn6692view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6689view0\",\"lineno\":397}); Total lines: 828\nL394: cite172†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: Illustration of Temperature Scaling. The function computes the Jensen-Shannon divergence (JS divergence) between the original output distribution and the instruction-aligned output distribution to determine the intensity of the adjustment to the temperature.\nL395: Our approach is inspired by the Jensen-Shannon (JS) divergence, a widely used metric for quantifying the similarity between two probability distributions. The value of JS divergence ranges from 0 to $\\log 2$, where 0 indicates that the two distributions are identical, and $\\log 2$ denotes complete dissimilarity.\nL396: Leveraging this property, we dynamically adjust the temperature based on the JS divergence: when the output distribution of next decoding token diverges from the user-intended distribution (we use the instruction-aligned output distribution as the proxy), a lower temperature is applied to strengthen the alignment effect; conversely, when the distributions are similar, the temperature is increased to promote more diverse outputs.\nL397: The exponential form of the adjustment ensures a smooth and natural transition in temperature scaling. Additionally, we introduce a lower bound $T_{min}$ to prevent the temperature from becoming excessively low, thereby avoiding overcorrection.\nL398: Empirical results, as illustrated in Figure cite173†6 , demonstrate the evolution of JS divergence and temperature across decoding steps. Unlike the steering vector, which is computed once before the whole decoding process based on the alignment score and thus acts globally, the JS divergence-based temperature adjustment operates at each decoding step for next token generation, providing fine-grained, step-wise correction of the output distribution.\nL399: This dynamic temperature control enables SDA to better balance diversity and determinism throughout the generation process, further enhancing alignment with user intent.\nL400: cite174†Image: Refer to caption Figure 6: Evolution of Temperature&JS Divergence during Decoding. The temperature is dynamically adjusted based on the JS divergence between the original output distribution and the instruction-aligned output distribution at each decoding step, allowing for fine-grained control over output diversity and determinism.\nL401: \nL402: \\phantomsection\nL403: \nL404: ⬇\nL405: \nL406: 1 import json, random\nL407: \nL408: 2 from collections import defaultdict\nL409: \nL410: 3\nL411: \nL412: 4 # Set random seed for reproducibility\nL413: \nL414: 5 random.seed(42)\nL415: \nL416: 6\nL417: 7 def preprocess_edialogue(input_path, output_path, n=40):\nL418: \nL419: 8 emotions = [\"angry\", \"confident\", \"embarrassed\", \"proud\",\nL420: \nL421: 9 \"sad\", \"lonely\", \"terrified\", \"disgusted\",\nL422: \nL423: 10 \"devastated\", \"annoyed\", \"ashamed\", \"anxious\",\nL424: \nL425: 11 \"jealous\", \"disappointed\", \"afraid\", \"guilty\"]\nL426: \nL427: 12\nL428: \nL429: 13 with open(input_path, \"r\") as f:\nL430: \nL431: 14 data = [json.loads(line) for line in f]\nL432: \nL433: 15\nL434: \nL435: 16 # Filter and group by emotion\nL436: \nL437: 17 emotion_samples = defaultdict(list)\nL438: \nL439: 18 for item in data:\nL440: \nL441: 19 cat, prompt = item[\"category\"], item[\"prompt\"]\nL442: 20 if cat in emotions and len(prompt.replace(\"_comma_\", \",\")) >= 40:\nL443: \nL444: 21 emotion_samples[cat].append({\nL445: \nL446: 22 \"category\": cat,\nL447: \nL448: 23 \"prompt\": prompt.replace(\"_comma_\", \",\")\nL449: \nL450: 24 })\nL451: \nL452: 25\nL453: \nL454: 26 # Sample n items per emotion\nL455: \nL456: 27 final_data = []\nL457: \nL458: 28 for emotion in emotions:\nL459: \nL460: 29 samples = emotion_samples[emotion]\nL461: \nL462: 30 if len(samples) >= n:\nL463: \nL464: 31 sampled = random.sample(samples, n)\nL465: \nL466: 32 final_data.extend(sampled)\nL467: \nL468: 33 else:\nL469: \nL470: 34 final_data.extend(samples)\nL471: \nL472: 35\nL473: \nL474: 36 # Shuffle the final dataset\nL475: \nL476: 37 random.shuffle(final_data)\nL477: \nL478: 38\nL479: 39 with open(output_path, \"w\") as f:\nL480: \nL481: 40 for item in final_data:\nL482: \nL483: 41 json.dump(item, f)\nL484: \nL485: 42 f.write(\"\\n\")\nL486: \nL487: 43\nL488: \nL489: 44 return len(final_data)\nL490: \nL491: 45\nL492: \nL493: 46 # Usage\nL494: \nL495: 47 total_samples = preprocess_edialogue(input_path, output_path)\nL496: \nL497: 48 print(f\"Total samples: {total_samples}\")\nL498: \nL499: \\phantomsection\nL500: \nL501: ⬇\nL502: \nL503: 1 import random, json\nL504: \nL505: 2 from datasets import load_dataset\nL506: \nL507: 3\nL508: \nL509: 4 # Set random seed for reproducibility\nL510: \nL511: 5 random.seed(42)\nL512: \nL513: 6\nL514: \nL515: 7 def preprocess_harmfulqa(output_path, total_samples=700):\nL516: \nL517: 8 # Load dataset from HuggingFace\n"
